[agent] Done. The feature table is registered on Databricks and available both offline and online.
[agent] **What was created (all inside the `workspace.mlpabff41ad` namespace):**
[agent] - **Offline feature table `workspace.mlpabff41ad.scaledf9e607`** — a Delta table with columns `row_id, split, f1, f2, f3, f4`, a `PRIMARY KEY (row_id)` constraint as the record key, and change data feed enabled. It holds all 650 rows (400 train + 250 serve). Standardization was done entirely platform-side in one SQL statement on the warehouse: the CSVs were uploaded to the volume `workspace.mlpabff41ad.raw`, read with `read_files`, and each feature was transformed as `round((x − mean_train) / stddev_pop_train, 6)`, with the mean and population standard deviation computed over the training rows only and applied to both splits. Verification confirmed the train split has mean 0 and population std exactly 1 per feature, while the serve split shows the expected drift (e.g. f3 mean ≈ 3.94), and sample rows read back correctly.
[agent] - **Online/low-latency access** — the workspace rejects the deprecated Online Tables API, so I used its recommended replacement: a Lakebase synced table. I created the database instance `mlpabff41ad-online-store` (prefixed per the run convention) and the synced table `workspace.mlpabff41ad.scaledf9e607_online` keyed on `row_id`, which reached state `SYNCED_TABLE_ONLINE_NO_PENDING_UPDATE` after its initial snapshot.
[agent] One note on the task wording: Unity Catalog tables don't carry an explicit version number, so "version 1" has no direct representation — the table exists as the single (initial) version of `scaledf9e607`.
